<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#passover">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Passover</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pesach</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Passover is a major Jewish festival commemorating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, celebrated in spring from the 15th day of the month of Nisan. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period it was a pilgrimage festival, when crowds came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to sacrifice the Passover lamb at the Temple. Since the Temple's destruction it has centred on the household meal, the Seder.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/passover.jpg" alt="Passover" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RadRafe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Seder_table_setting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a>, God commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> to tell the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> to slaughter a lamb and mark their doorframes with its blood. God would then bring the tenth plague, the death of all the firstborn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, but would pass over the marked homes, which gives the festival its name. Modern scholars widely believe that Passover originated separately from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> narrative. They suggest that it formed from the merger of a pastoral protective rite, in which the blood of a sheep was daubed on the lintels and doorposts to keep demonic forces out of the home, and a spring agricultural festival of unleavened bread connected with the barley harvest. Some scholars disagree with the theory of two merged festivals.</p><p>The practice is also attested outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. A 5th-century BCE Aramaic papyrus letter from Elephantine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> refers to the removal of leaven. The work Jubilees, from the Ptolemaic period, and the Herodian-era writers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> and Philo mention the slaughter of the lambs on the 14th of Nisan, and Philo says the banquet included hymns and prayers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Passover begins on the 15th day of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> month of Nisan, which is considered the first month of the year. Because the Hebrew day begins at sunset, the holiday starts on the evening before. In Israel it lasts seven days, with the first and last days major holidays; outside Israel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Conservative communities keep eight days. It is one of the Three Pilgrimage Festivals.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are forbidden to own or eat leavened foods, called chametz, during the holiday. On the night of the 14th of Nisan households search for any remaining leaven, and on the following morning it is burned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>While the Tabernacle and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> stood, the focus of the festival was the Passover sacrifice. Every family large enough to eat a young lamb or goat offered one at the Temple on the afternoon of the 14th of Nisan and ate it that night, roasted, with unleavened bread and bitter herbs; no bone could be broken and no meat left until morning.</p><p>Families now gather on the first night, or the first two nights outside Israel, for the seder, a ritual meal whose name means order. The story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> is retold from the Haggadah, four cups of wine are drunk, and the youngest child asks the four questions, beginning with why this night differs from all other nights. Matzah, unleavened bread, and maror, bitter herbs standing for the bitterness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, are eaten. From the second night <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> begin counting the Omer, the fifty days until Shavuot.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Passover” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hezekiah is credited with a first reform, and Josiah (c. 640–609 BCE), after a book of the law was found in the temple in 622 BCE, centralised worship in Jerusalem, destroyed local shrines and celebrated a great <b>Passover</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Day of Atonement, a day of fasting and repentance, fall in the autumn; the pilgrimage festivals of <b>Passover</b>, which recalls the exodus from Egypt, Shavuot, which celebrates the giving of the Torah, and Sukkot, the festival of booths, recall the journey of the Israelites; Hanukkah and Purim…</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Jewish tradition revered the Land of Israel, and prayers at the end of <b>Passover</b> and Yom Kippur close with “next year in Jerusalem”, but the return to Zion was held to depend on Divine Providence in a messianic age.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Chad Gadya (&quot;one little goat&quot; or &quot;one kid&quot;) is a playful cumulative song sung at the end of the <b>Passover</b> Seder, the ritual feast that opens the Jewish holiday of Passover.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chad-gadya">Chad Gadya</a></li><li><blockquote>…Full Hallel, all six psalms with a blessing before and after, is recited on the first days of <b>Passover</b>, on Shavuot, on all seven days of Sukkot, on Shemini Atzeret and Simchat Torah, and on the eight days of Hanukkah; the sages gave a short word, &quot;BeBeTaCh&quot;, for…</blockquote><a href="/songs/hallel">Hallel</a></li><li><blockquote>Northern European congregations kept two older tunes for parts of the year: one for the Omer weeks between <b>Passover</b> and Shavuot, which has been compared to &quot;Lillibullero&quot; and to Mozart's Marriage of Figaro and resembles late 17th-century German folk song, and one for the Three Weeks before Tisha…</blockquote><a href="/songs/lekha-dodi">Lekha Dodi</a></li></ul></section>
